Yes bro — this is **Edit Distance**, and your choices are almost there, but you have **Insert/Delete swapped in terms of which word index moves**.

Assume:

```text
Solve(i, j)
```

means:

> Convert `word1[i...]` → `word2[j...]`

Now look at the current characters:

```text
word1[i]     word2[j]
   ↓             ↓
   A             B
```

If they are different, 3 choices.

### 1️⃣ INSERT

We need to insert `word2[j]` into `word1`.

So we have now matched `word2[j]`, but **we haven't consumed `word1[i]`**.

Therefore:

```text
INSERT
cost 1
↓
Solve(i, j+1)
```

```text
word1: A B C
       ↑
word2: X B C
       ↑
```

Insert `X` before `A`:

```text
word1: X A B C
          ↑
```

We move in **word2**:

```text
(i, j+1)
```

---

### 2️⃣ DELETE

Delete `word1[i]`.

So we consume `word1[i]`, but `word2[j]` is still waiting.

```text
DELETE
cost 1
↓
Solve(i+1, j)
```

---

### 3️⃣ REPLACE

Replace `word1[i]` with `word2[j]`.

Both characters are now handled:

```text
REPLACE
cost 1
↓
Solve(i+1, j+1)
```

---

## 🧠 So memorize this

```text
Different characters:

INSERT
    ↓
(i, j+1)

DELETE
    ↓
(i+1, j)

REPLACE
    ↓
(i+1, j+1)
```

Your version:

```text
insert  → (i+1,j+1) ❌
delete  → (i+1,j)   ✅
replace → (i,j+1)   ❌
```

The key is:

> **Ask: which character did I consume?**

```text
INSERT  → consume word2 → j+1
DELETE  → consume word1 → i+1
REPLACE → consume both   → i+1,j+1
```

That's the AHA. 🔥


